<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chōsokabe Motochika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1539–1599</span> · Also known as Chosokabe Motochika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chōsokabe Motochika (1539–1599) was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of Tosa province on the Japanese island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>. Succeeding his father Kunichika in 1560, he unified Tosa by 1575 and by 1585 controlled most of Shikoku, relying on part-time farmer-soldiers called ichiryō gusoku. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>'s invasion of 1585 reduced him to Tosa, where he carried out a land survey and issued a house code in 1597.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika.jpg" alt="Chōsokabe Motochika" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E9%95%B7%E5%AE%97%E6%88%91%E9%83%A8%E5%85%83%E8%A6%AA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
